// EXPECT-DIAG: E102
// EXPECT-DIAG: Expected ';' after statement
// EXPECT-NO-DIAG: Statements are not allowed outside of a function
// Regression test: a typo'd keyword whose condition is parenthesized (so it
// parses as an expression-statement call, e.g. `iff(5 > 3)`, rather than
// hitting the "I don't know the word 'iff'" keyword-typo path some other
// shapes take -- see fail_bad_keyword.tr for that one) must fail with a
// single, clean error, not cascade into a second, unrelated, confusing
// error.
//
// Before the fix, this produced TWO errors: the real one below, AND a
// bogus "Statements are not allowed outside of a function" -- because
// ParseExpression() for the whole `fn main()...` returned nullptr
// NORMALLY (not via longjmp) after the nested body-parse failure, and
// main.cpp's top-level recovery loop had no handling for that case: it
// fell straight through with CurTok left mid-parse (partway through the
// malformed function's own body, not at a clean top-level boundary), and
// the leftover tokens (the rest of the body, its closing '}', etc.) got
// misread as new top-level content on the next loop iteration.
//
// This file only verifies the compile fails (test_runner.sh's fail_*.tr
// convention checks exit code, not error count/content) -- to confirm the
// cascading second error is actually gone, run this file directly and
// inspect stderr: it should show exactly ONE error block, not two.

fn main(): int {
    iff (5 > 3) {
        io::printline("hi");
    }
    return 0;
}
